{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC5 contradiction — measured, and the evidence AC5 now points at.\n\n`make verify` failed with exactly **1 failure out of 20,135 tests** (rc=2, 1h20m):\n\n    tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py::test_the_committed_baseline_matches_the_live_tree\n\nThe extra item is one baseline row:\n\n    autouse_fixture::tests/unit/reconciler/state/test_backend_scalar_surface_heldout.py::_clean\n\nThat names a file this story deletes, so the row cannot survive the merge.\n\n**Why AC5 as written could not be satisfied.** `scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` buckets the row `stale` and exits 0 — `active=647 new=28 increased=0 stale=1`. AC5's first clause therefore passed. But `make verify` runs a second, stricter gate the plan never accounted for: the pytest test above asserts `set(baseline) | admitted == live` **exactly**, with no tolerance for a stale row. So AC5 (\"`--update-stale` is not run\") and AC6 (\"`make verify` green\") were mutually unsatisfiable. No implementation could have met both.\n\n**What the drain does.** `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --update-stale` removes **exactly 1 row** — the one above, nothing else. Afterwards `stale` goes 1 → 0, `--check` still exits 0, and all **33** tests in `tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py` pass.\n\n**The mechanism is not being removed — the row is a duplicate.** This is the part worth recording, because \"a drained baseline row\" normally means a mechanism disappeared. It did not here. The surviving twin `tests/unit/reconciler/state/test_backend_scalar_surface.py:27-28` still declares its own `_clean` autouse fixture, and the baseline already carries a row for it at line 60. The two modules each declared an identical fixture; merging them deduplicated the declaration. The census count drops by one because one of two identical declarations went away, not because the mechanism did.\n\n**Why the drain is the sanctioned path, not a workaround.** `AGENTS.md` is explicit for this exact case: \"When your own change removes a mechanism, run `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --update-stale` in that change and commit the shrunken baseline.\" It equally forbids the alternative: \"Do **not** hand-edit `.github/mechanism-baseline.json`.\" The drain also cannot launder a regression — it \"refuses while any unadmitted mechanism is new or increased,\" which is why it is a ratchet rather than a freeze.\n\nSo the previous AC5 wording forbade the one path the repository's own guidance requires. AC5 is amended to require the drain; the diff is 1 deleted line in `.github/mechanism-baseline.json`.\n\n**Pattern note.** This is the second AC-versus-reality defect in this family, both in the same gate. The first was `new=0` (amended earlier; root cause bug `6f24-3d47-b251-4905`). Both came from reasoning about `--check`'s summary rather than running the gate the CI contract actually runs. The general lesson, which applies beyond this story: `check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exiting 0 is **not** the whole mechanism gate, and a plan that cites only `--check` will pass `make lint` and fail `make verify` 80 minutes later.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793501566076099062,"uuid":"22582b34-b2e9-4257-9754-1aafccd7fc03"}